.TH CB_FIND 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
cb_find \- Search for a key in a Critbit tree

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/cb_internal.h>

.IB const void*
.IB cb_find (
.IB critbit_tree* cb,
.IB const void* key,
.IB size_t keylen
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcb_find\fR function searches for a key in a Critbit tree. If the key is found, it returns a pointer to the value associated with the key; otherwise, it returns \fBNULL\fR.

The function takes three arguments:
.TP
.IR cb (critbit_tree*)
A pointer to the Critbit tree.
.TP
.IR key (const void*)
A pointer to the key to search for.
.TP
.IR keylen (size_t)
The length of the key.

The function returns a pointer to the value associated with the key if found, otherwise \fBNULL\fR.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcb_find\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/cb_internal.h>

int main() {
    critbit_tree* cb = cb_create();
    const char* key = "example";
    size_t keylen = strlen(key);
    const char* value = "data";

    cb_insert(cb, key, keylen, value);

    const void* result = cb_find(cb, key, keylen);
    if (result) {
        printf("Found value: %s\n", (const char*)result);
    } else {
        printf("Key not found\n");
    }

    cb_destroy(cb);
    return 0;
}
.nef